(a) Motorized carts may be operated within the corporate limits of the city by:
(1) Those persons who are 16 years of age and older and have a valid motor vehicle driver's license;
(2) Those persons who are 15 years of age, but not yet 16 years of age, if accompanied in the front seat by a person at least 18 years of age who holds a valid motor vehicle drivers license;
(3) No person under the age of 15 years of age shall be permitted to operate a motorized cart on any public street within the corporate limits of the city;
(b) All operators of motorized carts shall abide by all traffic regulations applicable to vehicular traffic.
(c) Motorized carts shall not be operated on sidewalks or in city parks other than in parking areas.
(d) Motorized carts shall be operated during daylight hours only except if such carts are equipped with functional headlights and taillights.
(e) Motorized carts may not traverse streets and highways under the jurisdiction of the state department of transportation. Motorized carts may cross these streets to access other streets at street intersections only. The specific streets and highways referenced include North Church Street, South Church Street, East Main Street and West Main Street throughout the corporate limits of the city.
(Code 2006, § 78-112; Ord. No. 02-01(1), § 9-8, 5-2-2002)

965:
(a) A motor vehicle shall be deemed to be abandoned or to be a junked motor vehicle in the following circumstances:
(1) When it is partially or completely dismantled or wrecked;
(2) When it is incapable of self-propulsion or of being moved in the manner for which it was originally intended;
(3) When it has been abandoned, or inoperable, or is damaged, and worth less than $50.00;
(4) When it is a junked vehicle and has been so abandoned for a period of not less than 30 days; or
(5) When it has been abandoned and does not display a current license plate.
(b) A motor vehicle shall be deemed to constitute an attractive nuisance or to constitute a health and safety hazard for the purposes of this article, in the following instances:
(1) When it is an attractive nuisance for children;
(2) When it is, or is reasonable likely to become, a breeding place for rats, vermin or snakes, or contributes to general circumstances which are inimical to the health or safety of human beings; or
(3) When it constitutes or is reasonable likely to constitute a fire and safety hazard and a source of fire and explosion, and is otherwise detrimental to the safety of human beings, especially of children, and of property.
(c) It is hereby declared that all such vehicles described in subsections (a) or (b) of this section which have remained on privately-owned property for as long as 30 days, are a hazard to health and safety and constitute an attractive nuisance for children and a public nuisance.
(Code 2006, § 78-151; Ord. of 11-9-2000, § 9-4)

